Concerning Frank's recent message about the revised picture
environment and Kristoffer Rose's forceful reply, perhaps I might
suggest that the two points of view are not mutually exclusive.  While
I (for one) would be more than happy for LaTeX3 to draw lines via
PostScripts specials, there is no reason why a style file cannot be
used to put these into place.  The same happens with the EEPIC macros,
which replace the line-drawing commands of EPIC with tpic specials.  I
seem to recall a similar style file which overwrites the line-drawing
commands of PiCTeX with tpic specials.

In fact, if producing the same output on all devices is deemed to be
of overriding importance, perhaps the default could be made to be
PiCTeX-like lines, with style files to use line fonts or PS lines
supplied as standard parts of LaTeX3.

At the same time, could we please have LaTeX specials that (a) insert
grey-scale images onto the printed page; and (b) specify the colour of
the text?  (People may remember that I raised this about 3 months
ago.)  These are probably the two major features that stop LaTeX
competing successfully with those dreadful DTP packages; and they are
certainly causing me pain in typesetting my image processing textbook!

.Adrian

